<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gruyères</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gruyères is a small medieval town in the canton of Fribourg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, on a hill in the pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarine">Saane</a> valley. Its castle was the seat of the counts of Gruyère from the 11th century until 1555, when their creditors Fribourg and Bern divided the county. The surrounding region gave its name to Gruyère cheese.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gruyeres.jpg" alt="Gruyères" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian David · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gruy%C3%A8res_et_sa_cit%C3%A9_m%C3%A9di%C3%A9vale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
